<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Subject–Verb Agreement - Common patterns</h1>
  <section class="summary-block summary-peach">
    <h2>Be, have and do</h2>
    <p>“He <strong>is</strong> ready”; “They <strong>are</strong> ready.”</p>
    <p>“The child <strong>has</strong> a pencil”; “The children <strong>have</strong> pencils.”</p>
    <p>“She <strong>does</strong> the work”; “They <strong>do</strong> the work.”</p>
    <p>In a progressive form, the helping verb agrees: “The child <strong>is playing</strong>”; “The children <strong>are playing</strong>.”</p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-green">
    <h2>There is and there are</h2>
    <p>Look at the noun that follows, not at <em>there</em>.</p>
    <p>There <strong>is</strong> one book. There <strong>are</strong> three books.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-lilac">
    <h2>Indefinite pronouns</h2>
    <p><em>Everyone, everybody, anyone, somebody, nobody, each</em> and <em>every</em> are singular.</p>
    <p>Everyone <strong>is</strong> happy. Nobody <strong>likes</strong> rain. Each child <strong>was</strong> ready.</p>
    <p><em>Few, many, several</em> and <em>both</em> are plural. Many <strong>were</strong> waiting. Few <strong>have</strong> finished.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-yellow">
    <h2>Either and neither alone</h2>
    <p><em>Either</em> and <em>neither</em> are singular when they are used alone.</p>
    <p>Neither of the answers <strong>is</strong> correct. Either plan <strong>works</strong>.</p>
  </section>
</div>
